  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 214 PM EDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis/Forecast: Early this afternoon, 994mb low pressure \nsits near James Bay with a warm front in the process of trekking \nacross northern MI. Trailing cold front not far behind, generally \ndraped across the central U.P. into northeast WI. Gusty southwest \nwinds remain the rule this afternoon with winds eventually flipping \nnorthwesterly and diminishing behind the cold front. By late evening, \nthis cold front should have cleared the area with cooler and notably \ndrier air filtering in as surface high pressure builds into the area \nby Wednesday morning. This boundary looks to turn stationary over \nthe lower Great Lakes into the Midwest Wednesday-Thursday on the \nnorthern periphery of building heat dome over the southern Plains \nand Southeast states. This should largely focus additional precip \nchances to our south through late in the work week before additional \nenergy treks over the nation's midsection into the Great Lakes at \ntimes this weekend through early next week. \n\nForecast Details: Gusty southwest winds and lingering showers remain \nthe rule early this afternoon, but both expected to diminish over \nthe next handful of hours behind a cold front surging east across \nthe forecast area later this afternoon through this evening. Winds \nturn out of the northwest area-wide by late evening/early overnight \nand markedly diminish. Cooler and much drier air aiding to partially \nclear skies through this time frame with tonight's lows spanning the \n40s to low 50s -- coldest across the interior areas of the eastern \nU.P. and north-central lower.\n\nAforementioned boundary that becomes stationary well to our south \nWednesday through Friday should focus the vast majority of \nadditional precip to our south leaving primarily dry conditions \nlocally. The one possible blemish arrives late Wednesday night/early \nThursday as quick-moving mid-level energy may be enough to squeeze \nout a few showers, but certainly doesn't look to feature much in the \nway of impact if it occurs at all. Seasonable high temps mid-late \nweek ranging from 65-75 degrees.\n\nThis weekend into early next week, lots of uncertainty prevails, but \nlatest trends continue to support renewed chances for periods of \nrain, potentially locally heavy at times as another potent wave and \nsurge of deep moisture set their sights on the middle of the \ncountry. Certainly a timeframe worth monitoring over the coming days \nfor how amplified that wave becomes and how far north the surge in \ndeep moisture makes it.",
